<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  The Win32 application manifest, embedded into Halcyon.exe.

  ## ASCII only, deliberately

  There is not one non-ASCII character in this file, including in these comments, and there must
  not be. The first version used em-dashes. The manifest then embedded as a resource that neither
  mt.exe nor the App Certification Kit could read - "An illegal character was encountered" -
  so WACK reported "Failed to process the binary" and then "the app is not DPI Aware", which is a
  confusing way to be told about a punctuation mark in a comment.

  The file is declared utf-8 and its bytes are utf-8, but the Windows manifest tooling reads the
  embedded resource as ANSI, so the three bytes of an em-dash arrive as three unrelated
  characters in the middle of an XML document.

  ## No double hyphens either, which is the trap under the trap

  Replacing those em-dashes with "-" + "-" fixed the encoding and broke the XML instead: a
  double hyphen may not appear inside an XML comment at all. The error changed from "An illegal
  character was encountered" to "Windows was unable to parse the requested XML data", which
  looks like progress and is a different bug.

  Single hyphens throughout. `tests/manifest.rs` fails the build on either mistake, because
  neither is visible in a diff and both surface a thousand lines away as a DPI warning.

  ## Why this file exists

  The App Certification Kit flagged two things, and both were real:

    "The app is not DPI Aware."
    "Windows applications must have a manifest defining its execution level."

  The first is the interesting one, because the app *is* DPI-aware - wry calls
  `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext` while it starts, which is why the window measures in real
  physical pixels on a 200% display. But that is a call made *after* the process begins. Until it
  runs, Windows believes the process is DPI-unaware, and anything it does in that window - the
  size it thinks the screen is, the first frame it paints - is computed against a virtualised
  desktop and then bitmap-stretched.

  Declaring it here makes it true from process creation instead of shortly afterwards. For a
  text-heavy application on a high-DPI laptop, the difference is whether the first thing anybody
  sees is sharp.

  ## What each element is for

  * `dpiAwareness = PerMonitorV2` - the modern one: the app is told when it moves between
    monitors of different scale and re-lays-out, rather than being stretched. `dpiAware = true/PM`
    is the legacy fallback for Windows 8.1 and is read only when the first is not understood.
  * `requestedExecutionLevel asInvoker` - run as whoever started it, and never ask for
    administrator. A mail client has no business being elevated, and `uiAccess="false"` says it
    does not need to drive other applications' windows either.
  * `longPathAware` - paths longer than 260 characters. A mail client writes files named after
    attachments, in a folder the user chose; both halves of that can be long, and the failure
    without this is a save that silently does nothing.
  * `activeCodePage = UTF-8` - the process code page. Mail is full of non-ASCII, and any Win32
    API called with an `A` suffix anywhere in a dependency would otherwise interpret bytes in the
    machine's legacy code page.
  * The `compatibility` GUIDs say which Windows versions this was tested against. Without them
    Windows applies shims for behaviour that changed in Vista, which is not what anyone wants.
-->

<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="com.uniki.halcyon" version="1.0.0.0" />

  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 share this GUID. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <!-- 8.1, 8, 7 and Vista, in that order. Halcyon requires Windows 11 and will not start on
           these, but declaring them is what stops the compatibility shims being applied. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{4a2f28e3-53b9-4441-ba9c-d69d4a4a6e38}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{35138b9a-5d96-4fbd-8e2d-a2440225f93a}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{e2011457-1546-43c5-a5fe-008deee3d3f0}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/PM</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
